[White "12) Asymmetric Benoni"]
[Black "Vladimir Akopian vs. Levan Pantsulaia"]
[Result "1-0"]

 { Vladimir Akopian – Levan Pantsulaia, Dubai 2013 Learning objective: This game illustrates White’s central break with e4-e5 and the winning attack arising from it. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 c5 3. d5 e6 4. Nc3 exd5 5. cxd5 d6 6. Nf3 g6 7. Bf4 { One of the purposes of placing this bishop on f4 is to disrupt Black’s natural development by attacking the d6-pawn. } a6 ( { In the event of the natural } 7... Bg7 { White has the option } 8. Qa4 Bd7 ( { but not } 8... Qd7 $2 9. Bxd6 $16 ) 
9. Qb3 { giving rise to a long theoretical line in which White scores an outstanding 70%. PH: In more recent times, } b5 $1 { has become popular here, giving Black good play: } 10. Bxd6 Qb6 11. Be5 O-O 12. e3 c4 { , or } 10. Nxb5 Bxb5 11. Qxb5 Nbd7 12. Bxd6 Ne4 { , and in both cases Black has good compensation for the sacrificed material. } ) 
8. Nd2 { Aiming at c4 (or sometimes e4) attacking the d6-pawn. One objective of this move order is to prevent Black from developing his bishop to the ideal g7-square. } ( { More common is } 8. a4 { preventing } b7-b5 { . However, compared to the lines after 7...Bg7, there is no check on a4 anymore. } ) 
Nbd7 ( { Black had the extra possibility } 8... b5 $5 9. a4 b4 10. Nce4 { with approximately balanced chances. } ) 
 ( { If Black follows his normal development with } 8... Bg7 $6 { then } 9. Nc4 O-O 10. a4 { and the d6-pawn is under attack } ( { or even } 9... Nbd7 9. Bxd6 $5 ) 
Ne8 { A sad necessity; e8 is a bad square for the knight. } 11. Qd2 $14 { With a pleasant position for White, as in the game Vaganian – Haik, Marseille 1987. } ) 
9. Qc2 Be7 $6 { Black has been encouraged to develop his bishop on an inconvenient square. Improving the location of this bishop with ...Bf8-g7 will cost Black two essential tempos. } ( { Probably better was } 9... b5 $5 10. a4 b4 11. Nce4 Nb6 { with level chances. } ) 
10. a4 O-O $2 ( { PH: Playing normal moves will land Black in a difficult position. It was necessary to play } 10... Nh5 $1 { , and if } 11. Bh6 { then } Ne5 12. h3 f5 { , intending ...Nf7 after which Black can castle. The position is strange but not bad for Black. } ) 
11. e4 Re8 12. Be2 { White’s position is visibly better, as his pieces are well coordinated towards the goal of breaking in the centre with e4-e5. } Bf8 13. O-O Ne5 14. Bxe5 $2 { Hoping to accelerate the desired e4-e5 break, but this exchange is positionally unjustified for two reasons: 1. White enjoys a significant spatial advantage and should not trade pieces unnecessarily. 2. White’s dark-squared bishop is very strong on the diagonal h2-b8, supporting the break and attacking d6, hence it should stay on the board. } ( { Stronger was: } 14. h3 $1 { Covering the g4-square and intending to follow up with Bh2 and f2-f4 gaining space and preparing either e4-e5 or f4-f5. Black is just not well prepared to prevent this break. For example: } Bg7 15. Bh2 Qc7 ( { Or } 15. Bxe5 Qe7 16. f4 Ned7 17. Bf3 $16 { threatening Rae1 followed by e4-e5. } ) 
16. a5 $1 { A precise move order, preparing f2-f4 and } Nc4 { . } ( { If } 16. f4 Ned7 17. Nc4 Nb6 { Black is holding the position. } ) 
Rb8 ( 16. Bxe5 Ned7 $2 17. Nc4 $16 ) 
 { 17.f4 Ned7 } 18. Nc4 $16 { The advantage is huge, due to the threats e4-e5 and f4-f5. } ) 
Rxe5 ( { It is generally not a good idea to play } 14... dxe5 $2 { as after } 15. a5 $1 { ± Black’s c5-pawn is practically isolated and very weak. } ) 
15. Nc4 Re8 { There now follows a sequence of preparatory moves. White wants to break with e4-e5, while Black would like to achieve ...b7-b5, and of course both sides want to prevent each other’s plans. } 16. f4 Nd7 { Covering the e5-square. } 17. Rae1 Rb8 { Preparing ...b7-b5. } ( { Better was } 17... Nb6 $1 { since the trade } 18. Nxb6 $2 ( { If instead } 17... Rb8 17. Nd2 Nd7 { when the e4-e5 break is hard to carry out, and maybe White should settle for a draw with } 19. Nc4 Nb6 20. Nd2 { . } ) 
 ( { Another option is: } 17... Rb8 17. Na3 Nd7 $5 ) 
Qxb6 $15 { favours Black as the central break loses strength. } ) 
18. a5 { This move is the standard reply against ...Rb8, since it makes ...b7-b5 lose most of its power. } ( { The reason is that after } 18. a5 b5 19. axb6 { Black no longer has a mobile pawn majority on the queenside. } ) 
Qc7 $2 ( { A better alternative was } 18... b5 19. axb6 Nxb6 20. Na5 Qc7 { although White has a comfortable position after } 21. Nc6 $14 ( { or } 20... Qc7 20. f5 $5 ) 
 ) 
19. e5 $1 { The desired e4-e5 break has been achieved under good conditions. Note that if White still had his dark-squared bishop pressing along h2-b8, Black’s position would just be lost. } b5 $2 { The decisive error. } ( { A better defence was } 19... dxe5 { although White has several good options, such as: } 20. d6 $1 ( { or } 19... b5 19. Bf3 $5 ) 
Qd8 21. f5 Nf6 22. Bd3 $1 Bg7 ( { of course not } 22... Bxd6 $2 23. fxg6 hxg6 24. Bxg6 $1 { +- } ) 
23. Qf2 $16 { With strong pressure down the f-file. } ) 
20. axb6 Nxb6 21. Ne4 { The threats Nxd6 and Nf6+ are decisive. } Bf5 22. Nf6+ Kg7 23. Qc3 dxe5 24. fxe5 { Black resigns. } ( { If } 24. fxe5 Red8 ( { or } 24. fxe5 Bd7 25. e6 ) 
25. Rxf5 $1  { the rook is untouchable: } gxf5 $4 26. Qg3 Kh6 27. Ng8 ) 
 { Final remarks 1. White’s bishop on f4 proved very useful, as it provoked Black to develop his bishop on the unnatural e7-square. 2. The central break would have been much stronger if White had not traded his dark-squared bishop. 3. Trading pieces often decreases the power of a potential e4-e5 break. For this reason Black had a great chance to equalize with the natural 17...Nb6. It often helps Black to trade off those pieces supporting the e4-e5 break. 4. After the break was achieved, Black’s position became very difficult and just one mistake was enough for it to collapse. Nevertheless, even with the best defence, White’s attack was very promising. } 
 1-0

