 { Christian Gabriel – Mikheil Kekelidze, Germany 1999 Learning objective: This game illustrates White’s thematic break with e4-e5 followed by f4-f5, gaining a powerful kingside attack. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 c5 3. d5 e6 4. Nc3 exd5 5. cxd5 d6 6. e4 g6 7. h3 Bg7 8. Bd3 O-O 9. Nge2 a6 10. a4 Re8 11. O-O Nbd7 12. Ng3 Qc7 13. Be3 Rb8 14. Qe2 { Both players have been following the theory relatively closely, and now we reach an unexplored position after: } h5 $5 { This move is a standard part of Black’s plan, intended to continue with ...h5-h4 undermining the protection of the e4-pawn. } 15. f4 $6 { This is premature, as Black can quickly bring pressure to bear on the e4-pawn. } ( { Better was } 15. Rfe1 { as Black’s plans are not so easy to carry out. For example: } Ne5 16. Bc2 Bd7 $2 ( { or } 16. f4 h4 17. Nf1 Nh5 18. Bg5 $14 { and the h4-pawn is vulnerable } ) 
 { Hoping for ...b7-b5 , but now } 17. f4 { and the knight is lost. } ) 
c4 $1 { The bishop on d3 is overloaded, defending e4 and preventing ...c5-c4. } ( { The alternative } 15... h4 $5 { simply transposes to 16...h4 after } 16. Nh1 b5 ( { or } 16... c4 17. Bc2 ) 
17. axb5 c4 18. Bc2 axb5 { . } ) 
16. Bc2 ( { But not } 16. Bxc4 $6 { due to: } h4 17. e5 ( { worse is } 17. Nh1 $2 Nxe4 18. Nxe4 Rxe4 $17 ) 
hxg3 18. exf6 Bxf6 $15 { White’s centre has been destroyed. } ) 
b5 $2 { This structure requires maximum accuracy from both players. In this case, this one mistake drives Black into a near-lost position. } ( { The correct order was } 16... h4 $1 { pushing the knight away. } 17. Nh1 { and now: } b5 $1 18. axb5 axb5 19. Ra7 Qd8 $15 { Black has a good position due to his mobile queenside majority. The break } 20. e5 dxe5 21. f5 { is simply met by } Nf8 { when Black is holding well, especially since White’s knight is not a threat from h1 . } ) 
17. axb5 axb5 18. Ra7 Qd8 ( 18... Rb7 { is met by: } 19. Nxb5 $1 { ± } ) 
19. e5 dxe5 20. f5 $1 { ± The reader should remember the sequence e4-e5 followed by f4-f5 as an independent break, an alternative tool in the position. White gives up a pawn in order to open the b1-h7 diagonal for his bishop, and free the e4-square for his knights. At the same time the e5-square is unavailable to Black’s knights, making the defence quite difficult. } Nf8 { Protecting the g6-pawn. } ( { Somewhat more stubborn was } 20... e4 { to block the b1-h7 diagonal, but after } 21. Ngxe4 b4 22. fxg6 $1 fxg6 23. Nxf6 Nxf6 24. Bxg6 { Black’s position is near to collapse, say after: } bxc3 $6 25. Bf7 $1 Kh8 26. Rxf6 Qxf6 27. Qxh5 $18 ) 
21. fxg6 fxg6 22. Qf2 { Exerting pressure down the f-file, threatening Bg5 or Nce4. } ( { Another winning move was: } 22. d6 $1 Qxd6 ( { or } 22. Qf2 Be6 23. Rxg7 $1 Kxg7 24. Bg5 N8h7 25. Nxh5 $1 { opening lines decisively: } gxh5 26. Bxf6 Nxf6 27. Qxe5 ) 
23. Bxg6 $3 { Leavin the h5-pawn without protection for: } Nxg6 24. Rxg7 $1 Kxg7 25. Rxf6 $1 { Which is winning after } Qxf6 ( { or } 25. Qf2 Kxf6 26. Nge4 ) 
26. Nxh5 ) 
 ( { Or: } 22. Nge4 $5 Nxe4 23. Bxe4 Re7 24. d6 $1 Qxd6 ( { or } 24. Qf2 Rxa7 25. Bxa7 $18 ) 
25. Rd1 Qb4 26. Nd5 $18 ) 
Rb7 { There is nothing better. } ( 22... Re7 { doesn’t help because of: } 23. Rxe7 Qxe7 24. Bc5 Qe8 25. Bxf8 Qxf8 26. Bxg6 $18 ) 
23. Rxb7 Bxb7 24. Bg5 N8h7 25. Nxh5 $5 { A very nice tactical resource. } ( { Even stronger was } 25. Bh4 $1 { threatening Bxg6: } g5 26. Bxh7 Nxh7 27. Qf7 Kh8 28. Qxb7 gxh4 29. Nf5 $18 ) 
gxh5 $2 { The final mistake. } ( { A better defence was } 25... Rf8 { but after } 26. Bxf6 Nxf6 27. Nxg7 Kxg7 28. Qc5 $1 { ± Black’s position is about to collapse. } ) 
26. Bxh7+ Kxh7 27. Ne4 $1 { +- With a crushing attack; the rest is very simple for White. } Bc8 ( { If } 27... Rf8 28. Qf5 $1 Kg8 ( { or } 28... Kh8 29. Nxf6 Bxf6 30. Bxf6 $18 ) 
29. Nxf6 Bxf6 30. Bxf6 Qe8 31. Qg5 Kf7 32. Bxe5 ) 
 ( 27... Nxe4 $2 { fails to } 28. Qf5 Kh8 29. Bxd8 { . } ) 
28. Bxf6 Qxd5 29. Qh4 Rf8 30. Qxh5+ Kg8 31. Qg5 Rf7 32. Ng3 Bb7 33. Nh5 ( { Black resigns, as the following lines are convincing: } 33. Nh5 Qd7 34. Bxg7 Rxg7 35. Nf6 Kh8 ( 35. Nh5 Kf8 36. Nxd7 ) 
36. Qh6 Rh7 37. Qf8 { Final remarks 1. Something to learn from this game is how delicate this structure is. If Black had only played the intermediate 16...h4 he would have had a somewhat superior position. Instead, the imprecise 16...b5 gave White a decisive attack. 2. White’s attacking scheme with e4-e5 followed by f4-f5 is often strong, but not always as effective as in this example. Black’s problem here was having played ...h7-h5 (weakening g6) in addition to the white bishop’s presence on the b1-h7 diagonal, which added pressure to the g6-pawn. } ) 

 1-0

