[Result "1-0"]

 { Nikita Vitiugov – Ding Liren, St Petersburg 2012 Learning objective: This game is an example of White’s attack based on the f4-f5 break. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. f3 O-O 6. Be3 c5 7. Nge2 b6 { This is a rare but reasonable continuation, as the move ...b7-b6 is useful in the pawn structure of this chapter. } ( { The main line is: } 7... Nc6 8. d5 ( { after the capture } 7... b6 7. dxc5 dxc5 9. Qxd8 Rxd8 10. Bxc5 { Black usually finds enough compensation after } Nd7 11. Be3 Nde5 12. Nf4 b6 $13 ) 
Ne5 9. Ng3 e6 10. Be2 exd5 11. cxd5 a6 12. a4 { With a position similar to Hellsten – Cordova, which is analyzed later in this chapter. } ) 
8. d5 e6 9. Nf4 exd5 10. Nfxd5 Nc6 11. Qd2 Nxd5 12. Nxd5 Be6 13. Bd3 Bxd5 14. cxd5 { Finally we have reached the structure of interest. Having two minor pieces off the board already, Black is doing quite well, and is likely to gain the initiative provided he proceeds precisely. } Nd4 $2 { This move does not quite fit with the position. Black’s main plan is to advance the queenside majority, but it will be difficult to push ...c5-c4 with a knight on d4. } ( { Much stronger was } 14... Ne5 { threatening ...Nxd3 followed by ...Bxb2 , and meeting } 15. Be2 { with } f5 $1 { . This break weakens White’s position while also neutralizing future efforts to expand with f3-f4 and e4-e5. A possible continuation is } 16. exf5 ( { or } 15... Nd4 15. O-O fxe4 17. fxe4 Qd7 $15 ) 
Nc4 $1 17. Bxc4 Qh4 18. Kd1 Qxc4 19. fxg6 hxg6 $15 { when Black has more than enough compensation for the pawn. } ) 
15. O-O b5 ( { PH: Modern computers prefer } 15... a5 16. Kh1 a4 { , grabbing some space and perhaps intending ...a3. The game could continue } 17. Rae1 b5 18. f4 b4 19. f5 Be5 { and Black has a pleasant position; it's not clear how White should get his attack going. } ) 
16. Kh1 Qd7 ( { To illustrate why 14...Nd4 was a poor decision, note that the desired } 16... c4 $2 { runs into problems after: } 17. Bb1 $1 ( { but not } 16... Qd7 16. Bxc4 $2 Nxf3 $1 18. gxf3 bxc4 $11 ) 
Qf6 ( 17... Qb6 $2 18. Rd1 $18 ) 
18. Bg5 Nxf3 ( { or } 18... Qe5 $6 19. f4 Qe8 20. f5 $1 { ± } ) 
19. gxf3 Qxb2 20. Bc2 $16 { Black does not have enough for the lost piece. } ) 
17. Rae1 b4 ( 17... c4 $2 { loses to } 18. Bb1 { . } ) 
18. f4 h5 $2 { This move is hard to understand. Is it possible that Black did not expect White’s reply? } ( { If } 18... Rac8 { the strong } 19. f5 $1 { ± gets back into trouble similar to the game, for example: } Be5 20. Bxd4 Bxd4 $2 ( 20... cxd4 21. Qxb4 $16 ) 
21. f6 $1 { +- } ) 
 ( { But the natural } 18... Rfc8 19. f5 Be5 { yields a complex position with level chances. The try } 20. Bxd4 Bxd4 21. e5 $2 { is simply met by } Bxe5 22. Rxe5 $2 dxe5 23. f6 Qd8 24. Qh6 Qf8 $19 { when Black is just in time to prevent mate. } ) 
19. f5 $1 { ± This is a perfect occasion to carry out the f4-f5 plan, as the g6-pawn is weak and White is ready to embark on decisive mating threats. } Be5 { The best continuation. } ( { Worse is } 19... Qe7 $2 { due to } 20. Bg5 Qe5 21. f6 Bh8 22. Bh4 $1 { (threatening Bg3xd6) } Qe8 23. e5 dxe5 24. Qg5 { with decisive threats such as Rxe5 followed by Bxg6 . } ) 
20. Bxd4 Bxd4 $2 { This mistake allows White to lock the bishop away from the defence. } ( { Necessary was: } 20... cxd4 21. Qh6 Bg7 22. Qg5 Rae8 23. f6 Bh8 ( { or } 23... Re5 24. Qd2 Bh8 25. Qxb4 $16 ) 
24. Rc1 $16 ) 
21. e5 $1 { +- Black’s d4-bishop can no longer protect the kingside, and the attack becomes irresistible. } Bxe5 ( { Or } 21... dxe5 22. f6 e4 23. Rxe4 { threatening Qh6 } ( { but not } 22... Bxe5 22. Qh6 $2 Bxf6 $1 ) 
Qd6 24. Rxd4 $1 cxd4 25. Qh6 $18 ) 
22. Rxe5 dxe5 23. f6 { Threatening Qh6-g7#. } Kh7 24. Rf5 $1 ( { Black resigns. The threat of Rxh5 decides the game since } 24. Rf5 gxf5 ( 24. Rf5 Qxf5 25. Bxf5 gxf5 26. Qg5 Rg8 27. Qxh5 ) 
 ( 24. Rf5 Rfd8 25. Rxh5 Kg8 26. Qh6 gxh5 27. Qg7 ) 
 { loses to: } 25. Qg5 Rg8 26. Qxh5 { Final remarks 1. The trading of pieces favours Black in this position, and Black could have proved this by playing the natural 14...Ne5 followed by ...f5 disrupting White’s centre. Black’s biggest mistake was to play 14...Nd4, because this knight needs protection. As a consequence, Black was unable to advance his majority in a natural way with ...c5-c4. 2. White’s f4-f5 attack worked particularly well because Black had weakened his kingside with ...h7-h5. } ) 

 1-0

