 { Pavel Eljanov – Teimour Radjabov, Wijk aan Zee 2008 Learning objective: This game illustrates Black’s queenside play, combined with the ...f7-f5 break, decisively undermining White’s centre. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Bd3 O-O 6. Nge2 c5 7. d5 e6 8. Bg5 h6 9. Bf4 ( { An alternative was } 9. Bh4 g5 10. Bg3 Nh5 11. O-O Na6 12. a3 Nc7 { with a complex position as in the game Navara – Dominguez Perez, Havana 2011. } ) 
Ng4 $5 { Keeping the centre undefined. Black reserves the possibility to continue with ...e6-e5 if convenient. } ( { An alternative was } 9... exd5 $5 10. cxd5 { . } ( { But not } 9... Ng4 9. exd5 $6 { as after } Nh5 $1 11. Bd2 Nd7 12. b3 Ne5 13. Bc2 Re8 14. O-O { the typical break } b5 $1 { ⩱ allows Black to take the initiative. This break is discussed further in the next chapter. } ) 
Na6 $5 { With a standard Benoni position, which is similar to the game. } ) 
10. Qd2 Na6 11. a3 Nc7 12. f3 Ne5 13. O-O exd5 ( { Black could convert this position into a King’s Indian Type II with } 13... Nxd3 14. Qxd3 e5 15. Be3 f5 $5 { with a complex structure which we will study in Chapter 15. } ) 
14. cxd5 { Black reaches the asymmetric Benoni under good conditions, as he will be able to trade some pieces to alleviate the space problem and to decrease the power of White’s central breaks. } ( { The strange-looking } 14. exd5 { deserves attention, though after } Nxd3 15. Qxd3 Bf5 16. Ne4 Qd7 { Black has a good version of next chapter’s structure, and chances are level. } ) 
Nxd3 { The right decision, planning to continue with 15...b6, when Black begins to fight for the initiative. } ( { Bad is } 14... a6 $6 { as after } 15. Bxe5 $1 dxe5 ( 15... Bxe5 { loses a pawn to } 16. Qxh6 ) 
16. a4 $14 { and Black’s queenside majority is no longer threatening, and White enjoys a lasting advantage due to his strong passed pawn on d5 . } ) 
15. Qxd3 b6 $1 { A natural decision; Black may follow with ...Ba6xe2 trading his least useful piece to then proceed with his standard queenside plans. } 16. Qd2 { Attacking the h6-pawn. } Kh7 17. Ng3 $6 { This awkward move was probably played in order to prevent ...Ba6xe2 trading the light-squared bishop. Unfortunately for White, his knight is doing very little on g3. } ( { An option was } 17. a4 Ba6 18. Rfe1 Qd7 { to be followed by ...Rae8 and ...f7-f5 , when Black is doing well. PH: Here White could continue } 19. Ng3 Bc4 20. Nd1 $5 { with a strong regrouping. Perhaps on move 18 Black should instead try } Re8 { , when } 19. Ng3 { transposes to the game, but } 19. g4 $5 { looks very strong for White (at least if you're a computer!). Exchanging with } Bxe2 19. Rxe2 Qd7 20. h4 $5 { also doesn't solve Black's problems, as there is no clear plan, while White can continue grabbing space. } ) 
Ba6 18. Rfe1 Re8 19. a4 Bc4 $1 { Black prepares to advance with ...a7-a6 and ...b6-b5-b4, or invade White’s camp with ...Na6-b4. The reader should notice how the bishop on c4 is by no means useless, as it disrupts the coordination among White’s forces. } 20. Nf1 { White realizes the bishop on c4 is too annoying, and decides to chase it away with Nf1-e3. } ( { If } 20. Be3 { , opening the way for the f-pawn, then } Na6 $1 21. f4 Nb4 $17 { when Black’s minor pieces severely undermine the coordination of White’s forces. } ) 
Bxf1 { The natural reaction. Trading minor pieces decreases the potential of White’s central breaks, while Black’s queenside majority becomes more threatening. } 21. Rxf1 a6 22. Bg3 Qd7 23. Rae1 b5 { The queenside majority starts to roll. White is only slightly worse, but in practice his position is difficult and psychologically uncomfortable as he has lost the initiative, and the desired central breaks are hard to carry out. } 24. f4 $2 { This premature attempt to accelerate the central breaks undermines the chain e4-d5, and will backfire against White’s centre. } ( { Safer was } 24. Kh1 f5 $5 25. exf5 gxf5 26. a5 ( { worse is } 26. axb5 $6 axb5 $15 { as the open a-file benefits Black } ) 
Rad8 $5 { when Black’s position is preferable in practice, due to the potential to create a passed pawn on the queenside. } ) 
b4 ( { Also strong was } 24... f5 25. exf5 ( { or } 24... b4 24. e5 dxe5 26. fxe5 b4 $17 { and the d5-pawn is falling } ) 
Qxf5 26. Rd1 b4 27. Ne2 b3 $5 { ∓ to be followed by ...Rab8-b4 and ...Qc2 targeting the b2-pawn. } ) 
25. Nd1 ( { Or } 25. Nb1 f5 $1 { ∓ is similar to the game. } ) 
f5 $1 { ∓ The reader should become familiar with this thematic break. After White has advanced f3-f4, the break ...f7-f5 can be very powerful, as the d5-pawn loses its support. In this game, Radjabov managed to carry out this break with perfect timing, and now the d5-pawn is unavoidably lost. White’s position falls apart. } ( { If } 25... Qxa4 $2 { White is in time to create counterplay with } 26. f5 Qd7 27. f6 Bf8 28. b3 { followed by ...Nb2-c4 with an unclear position. } ) 
26. exf5 ( { Worse is } 26. Nf2 $6 { due to } Bd4 $1 { threatening 27...fxe4 } 27. Kh1 Bxf2 ( { or } 27. exf5 Qxa4 $5 ) 
28. Qxf2 Rxe4 { . } ) 
 ( { Of course } 26. e5 { fails on account of } dxe5 27. fxe5 Qxd5 ) 
Qxf5 27. Ne3 Rxe3 $1 { This is the key, temporarily sacrificing an exchange in order to win the d5-pawn. } 28. Rxe3 Bd4 29. Bf2 Bxe3 30. Bxe3 Nxd5 31. Rd1 { After a series of forced moves we reach a seemingly calm position. White is a pawn down and it seems he might recover it by pressing down the d-file, but Black has an energetic response: } Re8 $1 { This enables Black to keep the initiative. } ( { Instead } 31... Nxe3 $2 32. Qxe3 Qf6 33. b3 { gives some compensation for the pawn, since Black cannot easily push his queenside majority. } ) 
32. Bf2 ( { A better attempt was: } 32. Qxd5 Qxd5 33. Rxd5 Rxe3 { . But the resulting rook endgame is an easy win for Black. } 34. Rxd6 ( { Or } 34. Rd2 Rb3 35. Kf1 d5 $1 36. Rxd5 Rxb2 37. Rxc5 Ra2 { as analyzed below. } ) 
Re2 35. Rxa6 $6 { } ( { Or } 35. Rc6 Rxb2 36. Rxc5 Ra2 { winning the a4-pawn and the game, since } 37. a5 $2 { loses immediately to } b3 38. Rc7 Kg8 39. Rb7 b2 { threatening Ra1 and b1=Q . } ) 
Rxb2 { Now material is equal, but the connected passed pawns give Black a decisive advantage, for example: } 36. a5 c4 37. Rc6 c3 38. a6 Ra2 39. h3 c2 40. a7 b3 ) 
 ( { The best defence was } 32. Bxc5 $1 dxc5 33. Qxd5 Re1 $1 34. Kf2 Rxd1 35. Qxd1 Qxf4 { when Black should be winning, but some technique is required. } ) 
Nxf4 $19 { There is no defence as Black is two pawns up. } 33. Qxd6 $2 { Making things even easier for Black. } ( { As IM Torrecillas points out, } 33. Be3 { is refuted by } Qg4 $1 34. Bxf4 Re2 35. Qd5 Qxf4 { . } ) 
Qe4 $1 ( { White resigns. There is no satisfactory defence against the checkmate threat on g2: } 33... Qe4 33. Qd7 Re7 35. Qg4 Ne2 { winning the queen. Final remarks 1. The reader should notice how Black did not clarify the situation in the centre until he had played ...Na6-c7 and was ready to trade some minor pieces. These two elements are helpful in this structure, hence Black’s opening strategy proved effective in this regard. 2. One of Black’s most memorable ideas in this game is 15...b6 followed by 17...Ba6 and 19...Bc4, to obtain activity for the light-squared bishop and ultimately trade this piece and only then push the queenside majority. 3. White’s biggest mistake was the reckless 24. f4, undermining the otherwise healthy f3-e4-d5 pawn chain. Black’s 25...f5 reply was rather obvious and very strong; it is a break to remember. } ) 

 0-1

