27. Ne3 Rxe3 $1 { This is the key, temporarily sacrificing an exchange in order to win the d5-pawn. } 28. Rxe3 Bd4 29. Bf2 Bxe3 30. Bxe3 Nxd5 31. Rd1 { After a series of forced moves we reach a seemingly calm position. White is a pawn down and it seems he might recover it by pressing down the d-file, but Black has an energetic response. } Re8 $1 ( { This enables Black to keep the initiative. Instead } 31... Nxe3 $2 32. Qxe3 Qf6 33. b3 { gives some compensation for the pawn, since Black cannot easily push his queenside majority. } ) 
32. Bf2 Nxf4 $19 { There is no defence as Black is two pawns up. } 33. Qxd6 $2 ( { Making things even easier for Black. As IM Torrecillas points out, } 33. Be3 { is refuted by } Qg4 $1 34. Bxf4 Re2 35. Qd5 Qxf4 { . } ) 
Qe4 { White resigns. There is no satisfactory defence against the checkmate threat on g2. } 34. Qd7+ Re7 35. Qg4 Ne2+ { Winning the queen. } 
