[Result "0-1"]

 { Johan Hellsten – Emilio Cordova, Buenos Aires 2006 Learning objective: This game illustrates how Black may combine chances of kingside and queenside play after breaking in the centre with ...f7-f5. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 c5 3. d5 e6 4. Nc3 exd5 5. cxd5 d6 6. e4 g6 7. Nge2 Bg7 8. Ng3 a6 9. a4 { Preventing ...b7-b5. } h5 $5 { This is typical when White’s knight is on g3. Black may later play ...h5-h4 to claim some space on the kingside and pressure the e4-pawn. } 10. Be2 Nbd7 11. Bg5 Qc7 12. O-O O-O 13. Qd2 Re8 14. f3 { This move serves two purposes: it supports the e4-pawn and it frees the f2-square for the future Ng3-h1-f2 manoeuvre. } Nh7 15. Be3 ( { PH: The computer suggests } 15. Bh4 $5 { , but it feels so difficult to make a move like this as you constantly have to worry about ...g5 trapping the bishop. } ) 
Ne5 16. Nh1 { So far both sides have followed the standard plans for this position. White now begins the typical manoeuvre Nh1-f2 to further support his centre and perhaps prepare the f3-f4 and e4-e5 advance. } Bd7 17. Nf2 ( { It is not a good idea to play: } 17. f4 $6 Ng4 18. Bxg4 Bxg4 19. Nf2 ( { instead } 19. f5 $2 { threatening h2-h3, runs into } Bxf5 $1 20. exf5 Rxe3 $17 21. Qxe3 $4 Bd4 $19 ) 
Bd7 $15 { Black is ready for ...b7-b5 , while White is unlikely to achieve a kingside break. } ) 
 ( { If } 17. h3 { (threatening f3-f4 trapping the knight) then } b5 $1 { allows Black to unleash his forces on the queenside after: } 18. axb5 axb5 19. Nxb5 Bxb5 20. Bxb5 Rxa1 21. Rxa1 Rb8 22. Bf1 ( { or } 22. Ra5 Rxb5 $1 23. Rxb5 Nc4 24. Qc1 Nxe3 $13 25. Qxe3 $4 Bd4 $19 ) 
Rxb2 $1 { This is the key move. After } 23. Qd1 Qb7 { it is White who must fight for equality, as the c5-pawn can advance and become a dangerous threat. } ) 
f5 { A standard rupture in this position, claiming space on the kingside and pressing against White’s centre. } ( { Now the usual } 17... b5 $6 { does not work as well due to } 18. axb5 axb5 19. Nxb5 Bxb5 20. Bxb5 Rxa1 21. Rxa1 Rb8 22. Ra5 $1 { when White is just in time to maintain the extra pawn. PH: Here Black actually has a funny resource: } Bf6 $5 { intending ...Bd8, dislocating White's rook. } ) 
18. h3 $6 { This is probably intended to cover the g4-square, preparing the f3-f4 advance. Unfortunately for White, the weakening of the g3-square is a far more important issue. } ( { Probably better is } 18. exf5 gxf5 19. Nh3 { followed by Nf4 with a good position, due to the weakness of the h5-pawn and the e6-square. } ) 
h4 $5 { A double-edged yet standard decision in this position. Black gains control of the g3-square, but the h4-pawn may become weak in some cases. } ( { An alternative was } 18... Nf6 19. exf5 ( { or } 18... h4 18. f4 Nf7 $11 ) 
gxf5 20. Rac1 Qa5 { with level chances. } ) 
19. Rae1 ( { PH: Compared to the previous move, } 19. exf5 gxf5 { is not attractive for White anymore as there is no Nh3-f4/g5 in the position any longer. } ) 
Qa5 { Threatening ...b7-b5 advancing the queenside majority. } 20. Ra1 { Preventing ...b7-b5 for now. } Rab8 ( { Of course not: } 20... b5 $2 21. axb5 $18 ) 
21. Qc2 $2 { The last three moves are evidence of Black’s success with ...f7-f5. Without the possibility of expanding on the kingside, White finds himself marking time due to the lack of targets to attack. } ( { PH: } 21. Rfe1 { is still slightly better for White, though Black should be happy to get a Benoni position like this. } ) 
Nf6 { Slowly heading to the g3-square via h5. } 22. Bg5 Nf7 $1 { This is one of my favourite moves in the game, and the reader should pay close attention to it. Black has advanced with ...h7-h5-h4 gaining control of the g3-square. White reacts with the usual Bg5 intending to capture this pawn, but Black’s move presents him with a dilemma: can he really capture the pawn? } 23. Bd2 $2 { This natural-looking move is a serious mistake. By not accepting the challenge, White cedes the initiative and his position becomes extremely uncomfortable due to the lack of targets. } ( { Necessary was the capture: } 23. Bxh4 f4 ( { An alternative is: } 23. Bd2 g5 $5 24. Bg3 f4 25. Bh2 Nh5 $13 ) 
 { After the text move, Black is threatening ...g6-g5 trapping the bishop, so now the best continuation is: } 24. Nd3 ( { If } 24. Bxf6 Bxf6 { Black’s control of the dark squares gives him a good position despite being a pawn down. } ) 
 ( { Or } 24. Ng4 $6 Nxg4 25. hxg4 Be5 $15 { when Black obtains a dangerous attack after ...Kg7 and ...Rh8 . } ) 
 ( { PH: White has the chance to complicate the position with } 24. e5 $1 { , vacating the e4-square. } Nxe5 25. Bxf6 Bxf6 26. Nce4 $14 { Suddenly White is quite active. } ) 
c4 25. Nc1 Nh5 $5 { Black has good compensation for the pawn, as he can continue to advance on the queenside with ...b7-b5-b4 , while White’s kingside attempts are currently on standby. } ) 
Nh5 $1 { Heading to the strong g3-square. } 24. exf5 $6 { The opening of the e-file only benefits Black. } ( { Better was } 24. Bd3 Ng3 25. Rfe1 Qc7 26. Bf1 b5 $15 { when White has a very uncomfortable position. } ) 
gxf5 { Of course Black should recapture with the pawn to cover the key e4-square. } 25. Bd3 Ng3 $17 { Black achieves a dream position for this pawn structure. His queenside majority has strong potential and may soon begin to advance. Meanwhile, his kingside pawns on f5 and h4, together with the strong knight on g3, are enough to neutralize all of White’ efforts to create counterplay in this sector. } 26. Nce4 $6 { Hoping to trade the knight on g3. } ( { A somewhat better defence was } 26. Ncd1 Qd8 27. Ne3 { hoping to gain activity on the kingside, but after: } Qf6 $1 ( { instead } 27. Nce4 Nxf1 $6 28. Rxf1 Nh6 29. Nxf5 Bxf5 30. Bxf5 Nxf5 31. Qxf5 Qf6 32. Qh5 { gives White some counterplay } ) 
28. Bc3 Qg6 29. Rfe1 Bxc3 30. bxc3 ( { or } 30. Qxc3 Ne5 $17 ) 
Qg5 $17 { Black dominates the board. } ) 
Qb6 $1 { A strong move, preventing White’s plan. Black’s advantage becomes decisive. } ( { Instead } 26... Qc7 $2 { allows White to solve his problems after } 27. Nxg3 hxg3 28. Nd1 { since the f5- and g3-pawns are weak, and the strong knight on g3 is off the board. } ) 
27. Nc3 ( { Instead } 27. Nxg3 $2 { loses a piece after } hxg3 28. Nd1 c4 ) 
Qc7 { Black does not even bother to trade his strong knight for the passive rook on f1. He plays according to the spirit of this structure, intending to advance the queenside majority with ...c5-c4, and ...b7-b5-b4. } ( { Even stronger was: } 27... Bd4 $1 28. Rad1 c4 $1 29. Bxc4 Rbc8 30. Bd3 Ne5 $19 { White’s position is desperate because he cannot release his pieces, for example: } 31. Bg5 $2 Bxa4 $1 32. Qxa4 Nxd3 33. Rxd3 Bxf2 34. Rxf2 Re1 35. Kh2 Rh1 ) 
28. Rfe1 { Freeing the f1-square for the bishop, which would otherwise be lost after ...c5-c4. } ( 28. Rfd1 { doesn’t help: } c4 29. Bf1 Qc5 30. Bc1 b5 { and the advance of the queenside majority is decisive. } ) 
 ( { Or } 28. Rad1 $2 c4 { when the bishop is lost. } ) 
 ( { Preventing } . Rfe1 c5-c4 { with } 28. b3 { is not effective after } b5 29. axb5 axb5 30. Rac1 { and now the simple } b4 $5 ( { or the more ambitious 30...c4! } 31. Ncd1 Ne5 { with a crushing queenside attack } ) 
31. Na2 { Nxf1 32.Rxf1 } Qc8 { is easily winning for Black. } ) 
c4 29. Ncd1 ( { The alternative } 29. Bf1 { is similar to the game after } Bd4 30. Ncd1 b5 31. Bc3 Rxe1 32. Bxe1 Qc5 $19 { when White’s forces are completely tied up. } ) 
b5 ( { Of course not: } 29... cxd3 $4 30. Qxc7 $18 ) 
30. axb5 axb5 31. Bf1 Qc5 32. Rxe8+ Rxe8 33. Bc3 b4 { Black does not even bother to capture the d5-pawn; he has bigger goals in mind. } 34. Bxg7 Re1 ( { A great final blow! White resigns. There is no satisfactory defence against the threat of mate in three. For example: } 34... Re1 34. Bf6 Rxf1 36. Kh2 Rh1 $1 37. Nxh1 Nf1 ( { Or } 34... Re1 34. Ne3 Rxa1 36. Qd2 Kxg7 { Final remarks 1. The critical moment in the game was after the move 22...Nf7, and White’s biggest mistake was not to accept the challenge with 23. Bxh4. 2. Once Black gained the initiative there was little White could do to fight back. This is often the case in the Benoni structure; the position is difficult to hold once Black’s queenside majority begins its march. 3. Black’s ...f7-f5 break is often well met by exf5 followed by a knight transfer to f4 (and possibly e6). If White does not take advantage of Black’s vulnerable king, then he is likely to fall into a passive position, as in this game. } ) 
 ) 

